@id     05_pointers
@title  Pointers
@topic  pointers
@lang   c

@theory
A pointer stores the address of another variable.

    int x = 5;
    int *p = &x;   // p points to x
    *p = 10;       // changes x through the pointer, now x == 10

Functions in C get copies of their arguments. To let a function change
the caller's variables, pass their addresses:

    void set_to_zero(int *v) { *v = 0; }
    set_to_zero(&x);

Your task: write swap so that it exchanges the values of two variables.
The program reads a and b and prints them after the swap.

Input:   3 5
Output:  5 3

@starter
#include <stdio.h>

void swap(int *a, int *b) {
    // exchange the values that a and b point to
}

int main(void) {
    int x, y;
    scanf("%d %d", &x, &y);
    swap(&x, &y);
    printf("%d %d\n", x, y);
    return 0;
}

@solution
#include <stdio.h>

void swap(int *a, int *b) {
    int t = *a;
    *a = *b;
    *b = t;
}

int main(void) {
    int x, y;
    scanf("%d %d", &x, &y);
    swap(&x, &y);
    printf("%d %d\n", x, y);
    return 0;
}

@test
@input
3 5
@expected
5 3

@test
@input
-1 0
@expected
0 -1

@hidden_test
@input
7 7
@expected
7 7
